<article class="env-example" id="0182">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0182">Example <span data-tag="0182">14.19.1</span>.</a> Suppose the category $\mathcal{C}$ has finite nonempty self products. A $0$-truncated simplicial object of $\mathcal{C}$ is the same as an object $X$ of $\mathcal{C}$. In this case we claim that $\text{cosk}_0(X)$ is the simplicial object $U$ with $U_ n = X^{n + 1}$ the $(n + 1)$-fold self product of $X$, and structure of simplicial object as in Example <a href="/tag/016E" data-tag="016E">14.3.5</a>. Namely, a morphism $V \to U$ where $V$ is a simplicial object is given by morphisms $V_ n \to X^{n + 1}$, such that all the diagrams </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ V_ n \ar[r] \ar[d]_{V([0] \to [n], 0 \mapsto i)} &  X^{n + 1} \ar[d]^{\text{pr}_ i} \\  V_0 \ar[r] &  X }  \]
</div>
  <p> commute. Clearly this means that the map determines and is determined by a unique morphism $V_0 \to X$. This proves that formula (<a href="/tag/0181" data-tag="0181">14.19.0.1</a>) holds. </p>
</article>